<div class="nbd-wrap">
  <h3>NBD Export</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Network Block Device</strong> — temporarily share a whole disk (or partition) over TCP so another
    machine can image it (e.g. to qcow2). This is <em>not</em> SMB/NFS (files/folders).
  </p>

  <dl>
    <dt>How this page works:</dt>
    <dd class="nbd-muted">Listener (server) · Image job (client) · Apply only saves settings</dd>
  </dl>
  <blockquote class="inline_help">
    <strong>Two separate actions:</strong><br><br>
    <strong>1. Start NBD listener</strong> — runs <code>qemu-nbd</code> on this Unraid: it
    <em>listens</em> on a chosen IP:port and offers one <code>/dev/…</code> disk as a network block device.
    Nothing is copied until a client connects. This is a process you start and later <strong>Stop</strong> —
    not a permanent Unraid “share service” like SMB.<br><br>
    <strong>2. Start image job</strong> — runs <code>qemu-img convert</code> on this Unraid as a
    <em>client</em>: it connects to <code>nbd://IP:port</code> (here or on a peer) and writes a
    <strong>file</strong> under <code>/mnt/…</code> (qcow2/raw). Survives closing the browser.<br><br>
    Typical lab flow: peer starts a listener on its disk → this Unraid runs an image job → stop the listener.
    Full walkthrough:
    <a href="https://github.com/ibigsnet/NbdExport/blob/main/docs/how-to-use.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">How to use ↗</a>
    ·
    <a href="https://github.com/ibigsnet/NbdExport/blob/main/docs/when-to-use-nbd.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">when / scenarios ↗</a>
  </blockquote>

